var documenterSearchIndex = {"docs":
[{"category":"section","location":"api/#API-Reference","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"API Reference"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Runs-and-configuration","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Runs and configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Saving-and-loading","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Saving and loading"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Storage-hooks","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Storage hooks"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Querying","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Querying"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Globs","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Globs"},{"category":"section","location":"api/#Table-formats","page":"API Reference","text":"","title":"Table formats"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#OutputVault","page":"API Reference","text":"OutputVault\n\nStorage of model runs: one folder per run, <output_root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<variant>/, holding a solution with the git state that produced it and result items saved after it, and queries across runs with DuckDB.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.RunHandle","page":"API Reference","text":"RunHandle(variant; branch = current_branch(), experdef = \"default\")\nRunHandle(; branch, experdef, variant)\n\nIdentifies a run, and so its folder <output_root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<variant>.\n\nvariant uses only A–Z, a–z, 0–9 and _ . + = -; a Symbol or an integer is converted with string.\nexperdef uses the same characters without -, so the folder name splits at its first hyphen.\nbranch defaults to the git branch of the active project's repository (current_branch). It may contain / (nested folders); no level may start with res- or .. Passing a branch chooses only the folder: provenance always comes from the active project's repository.\n\nThe names are checked when the handle is made.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.RunHandle"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.RunPattern","page":"API Reference","text":"RunPattern(; branch = \"*\", experdef = \"*\", variant = \"*\")\n\nRuns matched by globs, one per part of the run's identity: * matches within one level, ? one character, [...] one of a set of characters, and ** (in branch) any number of levels, so branch = \"**\" includes nested branches. Used by find_runs and query_results.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.RunPattern"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.current_branch","page":"API Reference","text":"current_branch() -> String\n\nThe git branch checked out in the repository of the active project (Base.active_project()), the default branch of a RunHandle. An error on a detached HEAD or outside a repository: pass branch to RunHandle there.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.current_branch"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.run_path","page":"API Reference","text":"run_path(rf::RunHandle) -> String\n\nThe folder of the run under the configured output root. It exists once the solution is saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.run_path"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Config","page":"API Reference","text":"Config(; output_root, table_format = nothing, native = true,\n         staging_root = joinpath(output_root, \".staging\"))\n\nA project's settings, made active with set_config! or with_config.\n\noutput_root: the top folder of the saved runs.\ntable_format: the format of table files, e.g. OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat(); needed only if a table is written.\nnative: also write native.jld2, the solution as Julia objects, on solution saves.\nstaging_root: where a solution's run folder is written before it is renamed into place; on the same file system as the runs, so that the rename is atomic.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Config"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.AbstractConfig","page":"API Reference","text":"OutputVault.AbstractConfig\n\nThe interface behind Config: the methods output_root(cfg), table_format(cfg), native(cfg) and staging_root(cfg). A project that computes its settings (the root from the machine, say) defines a subtype with these methods; only output_root is required.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.AbstractConfig"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.output_root","page":"API Reference","text":"output_root(cfg) -> String\n\nThe top folder of the project's saved runs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.output_root"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.table_format","page":"API Reference","text":"table_format(cfg) -> Union{AbstractTableFormat, Nothing}\n\nThe format of table files; nothing (the default) if tables are not saved.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.table_format"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.native","page":"API Reference","text":"native(cfg) -> Bool\n\nWhether solution saves also write native.jld2, the solution as Julia objects. Default true.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.native"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.staging_root","page":"API Reference","text":"staging_root(cfg) -> String\n\nWhere a solution's run folder is written before it is renamed into place. It must be on the same file system as the runs. Default <output_root>/.staging.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.staging_root"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.set_config!","page":"API Reference","text":"set_config!(cfg::AbstractConfig) -> cfg\n\nMake cfg the active configuration, used by every OutputVault function outside a with_config. Typically called in a project package's __init__.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.set_config!"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.with_config","page":"API Reference","text":"with_config(f, cfg::AbstractConfig)\n\nCall f() with cfg as the active configuration. The override holds only for f and the tasks it starts; other tasks keep their configuration, and nothing needs restoring when f returns or throws.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.with_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.global_config","page":"API Reference","text":"global_config() -> AbstractConfig\n\nThe active configuration: the one of the innermost with_config, else the one set with set_config!. An error if neither is set.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.global_config"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.save_solution","page":"API Reference","text":"save_solution(rf::RunHandle, solution; dirty_repo_check = :warn) -> String\n\nStart the run rf over with solution: everything saved for the run before is replaced. The solution is written at the path solution by the storage rules (a NamedTuple (; obj, stv) gives solution/obj.jld2 and solution/stv.jld2), the git state of the active project's repository in .provenance/, and, if the configuration's native is set, the solution as Julia objects in native.jld2, one key per member of a NamedTuple.\n\ndirty_repo_check (:warn, :error or :ignore) says what to do when the repository has uncommitted changes, or there is none. A dirty repository's staged and unstaged diffs (the latter with untracked text files, up to 1 MB) are saved in .provenance/.\n\nThe new run is written to a folder under staging_root, then renamed into place; the old run is renamed aside first and deleted last. Returns the folder holding the new run: the run's folder, or, if a rename or move failed (on Windows, open files can block them), the staging folder, with a warning that says where the new and the old run are. If only the final delete fails, the new run is in place and a warning names the old folder to delete by hand.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.save_solution"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.save_result_item","page":"API Reference","text":"save_result_item(rf::RunHandle, path, value) -> String\n\nWrite value at path in the run (levels separated by /, e.g. \"stats\" or \"irf/ss\") by the storage rules: a table file path.<ext>, a folder path/ for a NamedTuple, or path.jld2. What was at path before is replaced, a folder with everything in it. The run's solution must have been saved. Saving below an item that is a file (\"irf/ss\" when irf.jld2 exists) is an error. Returns the item's file or folder.\n\nA save that fails leaves the old item as it was. On Windows, if a file of the old item is still open (a Parquet file stays open after it is read until garbage collection), the new item is saved and the old copy stays beside it under a hidden name, with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.save_result_item"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.load_result_item","page":"API Reference","text":"load_result_item(rf::RunHandle, path; native = false)\nload_result_item(rf::RunHandle, path, T; context = nothing)\n\nThe item at path in the run.\n\nWithout a type, its data: a table file as a StoredTable (columns and file metadata), a folder as a NamedTuple of its items in alphabetical order, and a .jld2 file as a Dict of its contents.\n\nWith a type T, the item read by read_portable_jld2(T, io; context) for a .jld2 file, and otherwise from_storage(T, data; context) of its data. The default JLD2 reader returns io[\"value\"] without checking its type; a custom reader can validate or rebuild it. Even without a type argument, JLD2 reconstructs Julia objects stored in the file; only a custom plain-data layout avoids dependence on their original Julia types.\n\nload_result_item(rf, \"solution\"; native = true) reads the native copy (native.jld2, or solution/res.jld2 in runs saved with OutputVault 0.3) with JLD2 alone, and returns a NamedTuple with one member per key. native = true is an error for other paths.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.load_result_item"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.move_result_item","page":"API Reference","text":"move_result_item(rf::RunHandle, from, to)\n\nMove the item at path from to path to within the run, unchanged. An error if nothing is at from or something is at to. Neither may be under a reserved name (solution, native). On Windows, a Parquet file stays open after it is read until garbage collection (GC.gc()), and moving it, or a folder holding it, can fail.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.move_result_item"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.delete_result_item","page":"API Reference","text":"delete_result_item(rf::RunHandle, path)\n\nRemove the item at path, in every form. An error if there is none, and for the solution and the reserved names. On Windows, a Parquet file stays open after it is read until garbage collection (GC.gc()), and deleting it fails; a folder holding it may then be removed only in part.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.delete_result_item"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.list_run","page":"API Reference","text":"list_run(rf::RunHandle) -> RunDisplay\n\nWhat the run holds, read from its folder: the run, its provenance (commit, dirty or not) and a table of its items, the solution's included, with their paths, kinds and formats.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.list_run"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.RunDisplay","page":"API Reference","text":"RunDisplay\n\nWhat a run holds, as returned by list_run, printed as a readable listing: run (the RunHandle), provenance (a NamedTuple (; commit, dirty); commit is nothing if no commit was recorded) and items (a column table with path, kind (:table, :jld2 or :folder) and format, the file extension).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.RunDisplay"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.find_runs","page":"API Reference","text":"find_runs(pattern = RunPattern(); root = output_root(global_config())) -> Vector{RunHandle}\n\nThe saved runs under root that match pattern (a RunPattern), sorted by branch, experdef and variant. Reads only folder names, so no file is opened or downloaded. Folders whose names are not valid run names are skipped with a warning.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.find_runs"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.write_object_file","page":"API Reference","text":"write_object_file(path, x)\n\nSave one object outside any run, as a .jld2 file written by write_portable_jld2. The file is written to a temporary name and then moved to path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.write_object_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.read_object_file","page":"API Reference","text":"read_object_file(path, T; context = nothing)\n\nRestore an object saved by write_object_file as a T, with read_portable_jld2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.read_object_file"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.to_storage","page":"API Reference","text":"to_storage(x)\n\nThe value OutputVault writes for x: a Tables.jl table (one table file), a NamedTuple (a folder, one item per member; a NamedTuple of equal-length vectors is a table) or anything else (a .jld2 file written by write_portable_jld2). Default: x itself. A package adds methods for its own types.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.to_storage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.from_storage","page":"API Reference","text":"from_storage(::Type{T}, data; context = nothing)\n\nRebuild a T from the data OutputVault read back: a StoredTable for a table file, a NamedTuple of the members' data for a folder. OutputVault calls it only with a type the caller supplied. There is no generic method, so loading with a type that has none is a MethodError. context is what the caller of load_result_item passed. A .jld2 item uses read_portable_jld2 instead, without calling from_storage.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.from_storage"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.write_portable_jld2","page":"API Reference","text":"write_portable_jld2(io, x)\n\nWrite x into the open JLD2 file io that holds a .jld2 item. A package defines methods for its types to store a portable layout (plain arrays and strings that h5py, MATLAB or later Julia code can read without the package). Without a method for its type, x is stored as the Julia object, io[\"value\"] = x.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.write_portable_jld2"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.read_portable_jld2","page":"API Reference","text":"read_portable_jld2(::Type{T}, io; context = nothing)\n\nRebuild a T from the open JLD2 file io, as written by write_portable_jld2. context supplies information needed to rebuild the object, such as its model, as passed to load_result_item. The default reader returns io[\"value\"].\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.read_portable_jld2"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.QueryShape","page":"API Reference","text":"QueryShape(path_pattern; keys = (), column = :column, valuetype = Float64)\n\nA family of table items and the columns of a query over them.\n\npath_pattern: an item path in which a level {name} is a placeholder; its value in each matching file becomes the output column name. \"stats/{sample}\" matches stats/uncond and stats/boom.\nkeys: the table columns that identify a row, copied to the output.\ncolumn: the output column that holds the names of the table's other columns once the table is in long form.\nvaluetype: the type of the output column value (Float64, Float32, Int64, Int32, Bool or String); every selected cell is cast to it.\n\nquery_results returns branch, experdef, variant, <placeholders…>, <keys…>, <column>, value.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.QueryShape"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.query_results","page":"API Reference","text":"query_results(runs, shape::QueryShape; selections...) -> NamedTuple of vectors\n\nThe cells of the table items of shape in runs, in long form: one row per selected cell, with the columns branch, experdef, variant, <placeholders…>, <keys…>, <column>, value. runs is a RunPattern or a collection of RunHandles.\n\nSelections are keyword arguments named after a placeholder (which picks files), a key (rows) or the shape's column (the table's columns). Each takes a value or a vector of values; a String with *, ? or [ is a glob, any other value (a Symbol always) matches exactly. Globs apply to text only; numeric keys take exact values. Without a selection a column keeps everything.\n\nOnly files of the configured table format are read, and only those of the selected runs and placeholder values. Tables of different runs are combined by column name: a column that a file lacks gives no rows for it, and so does a missing cell. Text columns come back as String, numeric keys as stored, and value as the shape's valuetype; a cell that cannot be cast is an error naming its column. Rows are ordered by run, placeholders, row of the file and column of the table.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.query_results"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.vault_connection","page":"API Reference","text":"vault_connection() -> DuckDB connection\n\nAn in-memory DuckDB connection with OutputVault's macros loaded (vault_table_parquet, vault_metadata_parquet, vault_split_variant; see sql/outputvault.sql), for querying in SQL. Close it with DBInterface.close!.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.vault_connection"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.sql_path","page":"API Reference","text":"sql_path() -> String\n\nThe path of outputvault.sql, DuckDB macros that read lists of table files, for use from any language.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.sql_path"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.sql_statements","page":"API Reference","text":"sql_statements() -> Vector{String}\n\nThe statements of outputvault.sql, comments removed, for clients that run one statement per call.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.sql_statements"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.matches","page":"API Reference","text":"matches(pattern::RunPattern, rf::RunHandle) -> Bool\n\nWhether the run's branch, experdef and variant match the pattern's globs.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.matches"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.glob_match","page":"API Reference","text":"glob_match(pattern, name) -> Bool\n\nWhether name (levels separated by /) matches the glob pattern: * within a level, ? one character, [abc], [a-z] and [!abc] one character of a set, and a level ** any number of levels.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.glob_match"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.isglob","page":"API Reference","text":"isglob(s) -> Bool\n\nWhether the string contains a glob character (*, ? or [).\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.isglob"},{"category":"module","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats","page":"API Reference","text":"OutputVault.Formats\n\nRead and write Tables.jl tables with their file-level metadata, one file per table. These functions can be used without configuring a run. Load the format's I/O package before calling write_table or read_table (using Parquet2 for ParquetFormat).\n\nA format is a type F <: AbstractTableFormat with the methods extension, format_from_extension, write_table, read_table and, optionally, iswritable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.AbstractTableFormat","page":"API Reference","text":"AbstractTableFormat\n\nSupertype of table file formats. See OutputVault.Formats for the methods a format defines.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.AbstractTableFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat","page":"API Reference","text":"ParquetFormat()\n\nParquet files, written and read with Parquet2 (needs using Parquet2). File metadata are kept in Parquet's key-value metadata. Symbol columns, which Parquet stores as strings, are listed in an entry of that metadata and come back as Symbols. Other columns are passed to Parquet2 as they are; a type it cannot store is an error from Parquet2.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat"},{"category":"type","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.StoredTable","page":"API Reference","text":"StoredTable\n\nA table read from a file: its columns (a NamedTuple of vectors) and its file-level metadata (a Dict{String,String}). It is a Tables.jl column table (Tables.columns(t) returns the columns), and DataAPI.metadata reads the metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.StoredTable"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.extension","page":"API Reference","text":"extension(fmt) -> String\n\nThe file extension of the format's files, without the dot (\"parquet\").\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.extension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.format_from_extension","page":"API Reference","text":"format_from_extension(::Val{ext}) -> Union{AbstractTableFormat, Nothing}\n\nThe format whose files have the extension ext (a Symbol, without the dot), or nothing if the file is not a table file. A format's package defines the method for its own extension.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.format_from_extension"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.write_table","page":"API Reference","text":"write_table(fmt, path, table; metadata = Dict{String,String}())\n\nWrite a Tables.jl table to path with its own file-level metadata (DataAPI.metadata) merged with metadata. The file is written to a temporary name and then moved into place, so readers see either the old file or the new one. Column types the format cannot preserve are an error.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.write_table"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.read_table","page":"API Reference","text":"read_table(fmt, path) -> StoredTable\n\nRead the table at path, with its file-level metadata.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.read_table"},{"category":"method","location":"api/#Base.iswritable-Tuple{OutputVault.Formats.AbstractTableFormat}","page":"API Reference","text":"iswritable(fmt) -> Bool\n\nWhether tables can be written in the format (false for read-only formats). Default true. A method of Base.iswritable.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"Base.iswritable"},{"category":"constant","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.RESERVED_PREFIX","page":"API Reference","text":"RESERVED_PREFIX\n\nMetadata keys that start with \"outputvault.\" belong to the formats. A table's own metadata may not use them, and reading removes them, so the metadata read back is exactly the metadata written.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.RESERVED_PREFIX"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.table_metadata","page":"API Reference","text":"table_metadata(table, metadata) -> Dict{String,String}\n\nThe table's own file-level metadata (DataAPI.metadata, values as strings) merged with metadata. A key that starts with RESERVED_PREFIX is an ArgumentError.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.table_metadata"},{"category":"function","location":"api/#OutputVault.Formats.atomic_write","page":"API Reference","text":"atomic_write(f, path)\n\nCall f(tmp) to write a temporary file next to path, then move it to path.\n\n\n\n\n\n","title":"OutputVault.Formats.atomic_write"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frontends/#Storing-Your-Own-Types","page":"Storing Your Own Types","text":"Use storage methods to let users save and reload your package's result types. The collection of these methods, along with any save, load or query functions you provide, is called a frontend.\n\nFor an object stored as a table or folder, define to_storage to choose its representation and from_storage to rebuild it. For an object stored in its own JLD2 file, you can define a layout inside that file. Both approaches are illustrated below.\n\nA type that already implements Tables.jl can be saved directly; add from_storage to restore it as that type when loading.","title":"Storing Your Own Types"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frontends/#An-object-stored-as-a-folder","page":"Storing Your Own Types","text":"A small economy holds numeric parameters and settings. Store parameters as a queryable one-row table and settings as individual .jld2 values:\n\nusing OutputVault, Parquet2, Tables\nimport OutputVault: to_storage, from_storage, write_portable_jld2, read_portable_jld2\n\nset_config!(Config(output_root = abspath(\"../outputs\"),\n                   table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()))\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"frontend\")\n\nstruct Economy\n    beta::Float64\n    sigma::Float64\n    distro::String\n    mit::Bool\nend\n\n# a folder: a one-row parameter table (queryable) and the settings (.jld2 files)\nto_storage(e::Economy) = (params = (beta = [e.beta], sigma = [e.sigma]), settings = (; e.distro, e.mit))\n\nfunction from_storage(::Type{Economy}, data; context = nothing)\n    params = Tables.columns(data.params)\n    return Economy(only(params.beta), only(params.sigma), data.settings.distro[\"value\"], data.settings.mit[\"value\"])\nend\n\neconomy = Economy(0.99, 2.0, \"Beta\", true)\nsave_solution(rf, (; economy))\nlist_run(rf)\nRun main/res-frontend-base\n  commit: 1e0fef08a6e13ebed39a455c3d075e3ebff3c130\n  items:\n    solution/\n    solution/economy/\n    solution/economy/params.parquet\n    solution/economy/settings/\n    solution/economy/settings/distro.jld2\n    solution/economy/settings/mit.jld2\n\nThe outer solution is a folder, and to_storage(economy) gives another folder. Its params member is a table because all its members are equal-length vectors. settings is a folder of scalar values, so the raw values are under \"value\" in Dicts. The from_storage method above handles both:\n\nrestored = load_result_item(rf, \"solution/economy\", Economy)\n(restored.beta, restored.distro, restored.mit)\n(0.99, \"Beta\", true)\n\nThe parameter table can be queried directly, without reconstructing an Economy:\n\nshape = QueryShape(\"solution/economy/params\"; column = :parameter)\nquery_results([rf], shape; parameter = :beta)\n(branch = [\"main\"], experdef = [\"frontend\"], variant = [\"base\"], parameter = [\"beta\"], value = [0.99])","title":"An object stored as a folder"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frontends/#An-object-stored-in-a-portable-file","page":"Storing Your Own Types","text":"To make a JLD2 file readable without your Julia type definitions, store ordinary arrays, numbers and strings with write_portable_jld2. Define read_portable_jld2 to reconstruct the object. Leave to_storage at its default, which returns the object unchanged.\n\nHere the saved solution belongs to a particular model. The reader checks the model identifier passed through context before loading its coefficients:\n\n# a solution with its own layout inside a .jld2 file; the model comes from the caller\nstruct Solution\n    model::Symbol\n    coefs::Vector{Float64}\nend\nfunction write_portable_jld2(io, s::Solution)\n    io[\"coefs\"] = s.coefs\n    io[\"model_name\"] = string(s.model)\nend\nfunction read_portable_jld2(::Type{Solution}, io; context = nothing)\n    if !(context isa Symbol) || string(context) != io[\"model_name\"]\n        throw(ArgumentError(\"pass the stored model's identifier as context\"))\n    end\n    return Solution(context, io[\"coefs\"])\nend\n\nsave_result_item(rf, \"fit\", Solution(:dsge, [1.0, 2.0]))  # fit.jld2, plain arrays and a string\nfit = load_result_item(rf, \"fit\", Solution; context = :dsge)\nfit.coefs\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 1.0\n 2.0\n\nUse the same methods with write_object_file and read_object_file to save an object outside a run. The optional native.jld2 solution copy stores the original Julia objects directly and needs compatible Julia code to reload them.","title":"An object stored in a portable file"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frontends/#How-conversion-works","page":"Storing Your Own Types","text":"to_storage is applied to every member of a folder. On loading, from_storage is called for the type passed to load_result_item(rf, path, T). Its input is a StoredTable or a NamedTuple containing the folder's data. Convert any nested members inside that method, as the Economy example does for its settings. Loading a table or folder as a type without a from_storage method throws MethodError.\n\nFor JLD2 files, read_portable_jld2 receives the open file instead. Its default reads the value under \"value\", without checking its type. Define your own reader to validate the contents or rebuild the object from its stored parts.\n\nBoth readers receive the caller's context keyword. Use it for additional information needed to rebuild the object, such as its model. A reader has access to the item's own data and this context; save values that must be restored together as one item.","title":"How conversion works"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/frontends/#Save,-load-and-query-functions-for-your-package","page":"Storing Your Own Types","text":"Give frequently used result paths names in your package's API. A save_run function might save the solution and several result items; a matching loader can pass the appropriate type when loading each path. Define QueryShapes for your tables so users can select results without having to remember their stored paths and column names.","title":"Save, load and query functions for your package"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Table-Formats","page":"Table Formats","text":"Set table_format in Config to choose how tables are saved. Each table becomes one file, with its column names and file metadata. You can also read and write individual table files directly through OutputVault.Formats, without configuring a run:\n\nusing OutputVault, OutputVault.Formats, Parquet2, Tables, DataAPI\nwrite_table(ParquetFormat(), \"t.parquet\", (x = [1.0, 2.0], name = [:a, :b]); metadata = Dict(\"source\" => \"model\"))\nt = read_table(ParquetFormat(), \"t.parquet\")     # a StoredTable: Tables.columns(t).x, DataAPI.metadata(t, \"source\")","title":"Table Formats"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#What-every-format-keeps","page":"Table Formats","text":"File-level string metadata: the table's own (DataAPI.metadata) merged with the metadata argument. Keys starting with outputvault. are reserved for the format's own entries: writing them is an error, and reading removes them, so the metadata read back is the metadata written. Column metadata is not kept.\nColumn types: whatever the format's I/O package stores. A type it cannot store is an error from that package.\nAtomic writes: the file is written to a temporary name and then moved into place.\n\nReading returns a StoredTable, its columns with the file's metadata.","title":"What every format keeps"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Parquet","page":"Table Formats","text":"ParquetFormat needs using Parquet2, which loads the extension that reads and writes it. It keeps metadata in the Parquet file. Symbol columns are stored as strings and restored as Symbols by the Julia reader. DuckDB, Python, R and MATLAB can read the files directly.\n\nWithout Parquet2 loaded, writing or reading a Parquet table is a MethodError with a hint to load Parquet2. The metadata restoration of Symbol columns applies to the Julia reader; queries return text as strings.","title":"Parquet"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/tables/#Adding-a-format","page":"Table Formats","text":"A format is a type with four methods:\n\nusing OutputVault, OutputVault.Formats\nimport OutputVault.Formats: extension, format_from_extension, write_table, read_table\n\nstruct MyFormat <: AbstractTableFormat end\nextension(::MyFormat) = \"myext\"\nformat_from_extension(::Val{:myext}) = MyFormat()      # files *.myext are tables of this format\n\nfunction write_table(::MyFormat, path, table; metadata = Dict{String,String}())\n    meta = Formats.table_metadata(table, metadata)       # the table's metadata merged with the argument\n    Formats.atomic_write(path) do tmp\n        MyIOPackage.write(tmp, table; metadata = meta)\n    end\nend\nfunction read_table(::MyFormat, path)\n    meta = filter(kv -> !startswith(first(kv), Formats.RESERVED_PREFIX), MyIOPackage.metadata(path))\n    return Formats.StoredTable(MyIOPackage.columns(path), meta)   # the format's own entries removed\nend\n\nBase.iswritable(::MyFormat) may return false for a format that is only read. format_from_extension is what makes a file a table file when a run is read: define it only in the package that defines the format. Adding file I/O does not add a DuckDB reader: queries currently support Parquet only.","title":"Adding a format"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Quick-Start","page":"Quick Start","text":"This tutorial saves two calibrations of a model, adds summary statistics and compares their means. The experiment is called v1, and its two parameter choices are base and alt. The values are illustrative; substitute your model's solutions and statistics when using this in your own work.\n\nInstall OutputVault and Parquet2 as shown on the home page. Run this page's blocks in order in a Julia project inside a git repository. The branch and recorded commit come from that project's repository. On a detached HEAD or outside a repository, pass branch = \"demo\" to both handles below. Saving outside a repository warns and still works.","title":"Quick Start"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Configure-and-name-a-run","page":"Quick Start","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2, Tables\n\nroot = abspath(\"../outputs\")       # outside the repository, so saved runs leave it clean\nset_config!(Config(output_root = root,\n                   table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()))   # tables as Parquet files\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; experdef = \"v1\")       # branch: the current git branch\nRunHandle(branch = \"main\", experdef = \"v1\", variant = \"base\")\n\nA run is one folder, <root>/<branch>/res-v1-base/, named by the three parts of the RunHandle: the git branch, the experiment definition (experdef) and the variant (Runs and Storage). Making the handle creates no files. Repeating this tutorial with the same root and names replaces its previous runs; choose another experiment name to keep both versions.","title":"Configure and name a run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Save-a-solution","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_solution(rf, (; params = (α = 0.33, β = 0.96), policy = [1.0, 1.1, 1.2]))\nlist_run(rf)\nRun main/res-v1-base\n  commit: 1e0fef08a6e13ebed39a455c3d075e3ebff3c130\n  items:\n    solution/\n    solution/params/\n    solution/params/α.jld2\n    solution/params/β.jld2\n    solution/policy.jld2\n\nsave_solution starts the run, replacing anything saved under this run's name. Here, params becomes a folder of parameter files, and the policy vector is saved in solution/policy.jld2. The Runs and Storage guide explains how values become files and folders.\n\nThe run also contains .provenance/git_commit.txt, with the code's commit, and native.jld2, with a copy of the complete solution for reloading in Julia.","title":"Save a solution"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Add-results","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_result_item(rf, \"stats/uncond\", (variable = [\"K\", \"C\"], mean = [3.5, 1.2], std = [0.4, 0.1]))\nsave_result_item(rf, \"stats/boom\", (variable = [\"K\", \"C\"], mean = [3.9, 1.3], std = [0.3, 0.1]))\nsave_result_item(rf, \"notes\", (; text = \"first try\", converged = true))\nlist_run(rf)\nRun main/res-v1-base\n  commit: 1e0fef08a6e13ebed39a455c3d075e3ebff3c130\n  items:\n    notes/\n    notes/converged.jld2\n    notes/text.jld2\n    solution/\n    solution/params/\n    solution/params/α.jld2\n    solution/params/β.jld2\n    solution/policy.jld2\n    stats/\n    stats/boom.parquet\n    stats/uncond.parquet\n\nEach save_result_item adds a result at the given path. The statistics are NamedTuples of equal-length vectors, so they become Parquet tables; notes becomes a folder of JLD2 files. Saving stats/boom again replaces that sample. Saving at stats replaces all samples in the folder.","title":"Add results"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Load","page":"Quick Start","text":"stats = load_result_item(rf, \"stats/uncond\")\nTables.columns(stats).mean\n2-element Vector{Float64}:\n 3.5\n 1.2\n\nPass a type to read one value from a .jld2 file:\n\nload_result_item(rf, \"notes/text\", String)\n\"first try\"\n\nWithout a type, the file's raw contents load. The default .jld2 writer stores a value under the key \"value\", so the notes folder loads as a NamedTuple of Dicts:\n\nload_result_item(rf, \"notes\")\n(converged = Dict{String, Any}(\"value\" => true), text = Dict{String, Any}(\"value\" => \"first try\"))\n\nTo resume with the same Julia code, load the native solution:\n\nsaved = load_result_item(rf, \"solution\"; native = true)\nsaved.params.β, saved.policy\n(0.96, [1.0, 1.1, 1.2])\n\nThe statistics load as a StoredTable, which you can read through Tables.jl. To restore a custom result type, pass its type to load_result_item; see Your own type.","title":"Load"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Query-across-runs","page":"Quick Start","text":"A second run of the same experiment:\n\nalt = RunHandle(\"alt\"; experdef = \"v1\")\nsave_solution(alt, (; params = (α = 0.30, β = 0.96)))\nsave_result_item(alt, \"stats/uncond\", (variable = [\"K\", \"C\"], mean = [3.1, 1.1], std = [0.5, 0.2]))\nfind_runs(RunPattern(experdef = \"v1\"))\n2-element Vector{RunHandle}:\n RunHandle(branch = \"main\", experdef = \"v1\", variant = \"alt\")\n RunHandle(branch = \"main\", experdef = \"v1\", variant = \"base\")\n\nA QueryShape describes a family of tables: here every table under stats/, with the sample name from the file name, rows identified by variable, and the other columns (the statistics) turned into rows of a column stat:\n\nshape = QueryShape(\"stats/{sample}\"; keys = (:variable,), column = :stat)\nquery_results(RunPattern(experdef = \"v1\"), shape; sample = :uncond, stat = :mean)\n(branch = [\"main\", \"main\", \"main\", \"main\"], experdef = [\"v1\", \"v1\", \"v1\", \"v1\"], variant = [\"alt\", \"alt\", \"base\", \"base\"], sample = [\"uncond\", \"uncond\", \"uncond\", \"uncond\"], variable = [\"K\", \"C\", \"K\", \"C\"], stat = [\"mean\", \"mean\", \"mean\", \"mean\"], value = [3.1, 1.1, 3.5, 1.2])\n\nThere are two rows per run, one for K and one for C. The sample column comes from the path, variable from the table's key column, and stat from its column names. Without stat = :mean, each variable also contributes a row for std. Select one key with variable = :C, or include nested git branches with RunPattern(branch = \"**\", experdef = \"v1\").\n\nquery_results reads only the files of the selected runs and samples, with DuckDB, and returns a column table (Queries).","title":"Query across runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Saving-a-solution-starts-the-run-over","page":"Quick Start","text":"save_solution(rf, (; params = (α = 0.33, β = 0.95)))\nlist_run(rf).items.path\n4-element Vector{String}:\n \"solution\"\n \"solution/params\"\n \"solution/params/α\"\n \"solution/params/β\"\n\nThe previous statistics and notes have been removed. Recompute and save the results for the new solution, or use a different variant name to keep both solutions and their results.","title":"Saving a solution starts the run over"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/quickstart/#Your-own-type","page":"Quick Start","text":"For a custom summary type, define a Tables.jl interface and a from_storage method. This lets you save it as a table and load it back as a Summary, retaining its label in the file's metadata:\n\nusing DataAPI\n\nstruct Summary\n    label::String\n    variable::Vector{String}\n    mean::Vector{Float64}\nend\n\n# a Tables.jl table with a label in its file-level metadata\nTables.istable(::Type{Summary}) = true\nTables.columnaccess(::Type{Summary}) = true\nTables.columns(s::Summary) = (variable = s.variable, mean = s.mean)\nDataAPI.metadatasupport(::Type{Summary}) = (read = true, write = false)\nDataAPI.metadatakeys(::Summary) = (\"label\",)\nDataAPI.metadata(s::Summary, ::AbstractString; style = false) = s.label\n\n# rebuild it from the data OutputVault reads back\nOutputVault.from_storage(::Type{Summary}, data; context = nothing) =\n    Summary(DataAPI.metadata(data, \"label\"), Tables.columns(data).variable, Tables.columns(data).mean)\n\nsave_result_item(rf, \"summary\", Summary(\"baseline\", [\"K\", \"C\"], [3.4, 1.19]))\ns = load_result_item(rf, \"summary\", Summary)\n(s.label, s.mean)\n(\"baseline\", [3.4, 1.19])\n\nSummary is a table, so it is saved as one, with its metadata; from_storage turns the table read back into a Summary. A type that is not a table can define to_storage to say what to save instead, or write_portable_jld2 and read_portable_jld2 for its own layout inside a .jld2 file (Storing Your Own Types).","title":"Your own type"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/queries/#Queries","page":"Queries","text":"Use query_results to collect the same statistics across model variants, samples or git branches. The result is a table with one row per selected value, labeled by the run and item it came from. A QueryShape describes the saved tables; keyword arguments select the values to read.","title":"Queries"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/queries/#Shapes","page":"Queries","text":"A QueryShape describes one family of table items and fixes the columns of the output:\n\nQueryShape(path_pattern; keys = (), column = :column, valuetype = Float64)\n\npath_pattern is an item path in which a level {name} is a placeholder: \"stats/{sample}\" matches stats/uncond and stats/boom, and the file's name becomes the value of an output column sample.\nkeys are the table's columns that identify a row; they are copied to the output.\nThe table's other columns become rows: their names go in the output column column, their cells in value, cast to valuetype.\n\nvaluetype may be Float64 (the default), Float32, Int64, Int32, Bool or String. All output column names must be distinct, including the run columns and value. Paths omit extensions; each placeholder occupies a whole level. A fixed path such as \"params/params\" needs no placeholder.\n\nTake the statistics table stats/uncond.parquet of a run:\n\nvariable  mean  std\nc         0.71  0.02\nk         2.90  0.11\n\nWith QueryShape(\"stats/{sample}\"; keys = (:variable,), column = :stat) the output is\n\nbranch  experdef  variant  sample  variable  stat  value\nmain    v1        base     uncond  c         mean  0.71\nmain    v1        base     uncond  c         std   0.02\nmain    v1        base     uncond  k         mean  2.90\nmain    v1        base     uncond  k         std   0.11\n\nand in general branch, experdef, variant, <placeholders…>, <keys…>, <column>, value, as a column table (a NamedTuple of vectors). Text columns are Strings and numeric keys come back as stored. Rows are ordered by run, placeholder values, row of the file and column of the table.","title":"Shapes"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/queries/#Selections","page":"Queries","text":"shape = QueryShape(\"stats/{sample}\"; keys = (:variable,), column = :stat)\nquery_results(RunPattern(experdef = \"v1\"), shape; sample = [:uncond, :boom], variable = \"r_*\", stat = :mean)\n\nFor an impulse-response table whose rows are identified by shock, statistic and period:\n\nirfshape = QueryShape(\"irf/{start}/stats\";\n                     keys = (:__shock, :__stat, :__period), column = :variable)\nquery_results(RunPattern(experdef = \"v1\"), irfshape;\n              start = :ss, __shock = :shock_5, __stat = :mean, __period = [2, 3], variable = :C)\n\nThe first argument is a RunPattern or a collection of RunHandles. Each keyword selects on an output column: a placeholder picks files, a key picks rows, and column picks the table's columns. A selection is a value or a vector of values. A String with *, ? or [ is a glob; any other value, and every Symbol, matches exactly. Globs apply to text; numeric keys take exact values. A keyword that is not a placeholder, a key or the shape's column is an error that lists the allowed names.\n\nA run or a placeholder value without a matching file contributes no rows, and so does a cell that is missing. Tables of different runs are combined by column name: a statistic that a run's table lacks gives no rows for that run. A stored NaN stays NaN. A selected cell that cannot be cast to valuetype is an error that names its column; select the columns that can be cast, or query with valuetype = String.","title":"Selections"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/queries/#What-is-read","page":"Queries","text":"Selecting runs and placeholders limits which files are opened. For example, sample = :uncond reads the unconditional statistics and leaves other sample files alone. This is useful when your output folder is synced through a service such as Dropbox: only the selected files need to be downloaded. Filtering rows or columns still requires reading those files in full.\n\nQueries read tables in the configured table format. JLD2 files cannot be queried this way.","title":"What is read"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/queries/#SQL-for-other-languages","page":"Queries","text":"sql/outputvault.sql (OutputVault.sql_path) holds DuckDB macros that read a list of table files, for any language with DuckDB; vault_connection returns a Julia connection with them loaded:\n\nMacro Returns\nvault_table_parquet(files) the tables of a list of Parquet files, with __file and __row\nvault_metadata_parquet(files) their file-level metadata: file, key, value\nvault_split_variant(variant, regex, names) the variant split into named parts by a regex\n\nThe caller finds the files by the layout of Runs and Storage. In Python, the statistics of all runs of experiment v1:\n\nimport duckdb, pathlib\nroot = pathlib.Path(\"/path/to/outputs\")\nfiles = [str(f) for f in root.glob(\"**/res-v1-*/stats/*.parquet\")]\nfiles = [f for f in files if not any(p.startswith(\".\") for p in pathlib.Path(f).relative_to(root).parts)]\ncon = duckdb.connect()\ncon.execute(pathlib.Path(\"/path/to/outputvault.sql\").read_text())  # file at OutputVault.sql_path() in Julia\nif files:\n    rows = con.execute(\"SELECT * FROM vault_table_parquet(?)\", [files]).fetchall()\n\nThese macros return the stored columns plus __file and __row; unlike Julia's query_results, they do not add run labels or turn the cells into long form. The example skips hidden staging and trash folders. Find the files before querying, so DuckDB opens only the selected ones.\n\nIf your variant names encode several choices, vault_split_variant separates them with a regular expression. For example, UNNEST(vault_split_variant(variant, '^([a-z]+)(\\d*)$', ['scheme', 'level'])) splits capbail85 into capbail and 85.","title":"SQL for other languages"},{"category":"section","location":"#OutputVault.jl","page":"Home","text":"OutputVault stores the solutions and results of research model runs. Each run has a folder named by its git branch, experiment and variant, with a record of the code that produced it.\n\nUse it to save a solution, add results as you compute them, and compare those results across runs. For example, you can collect unconditional statistics for several calibrations, then query the mean of one variable in every calibration without loading the solutions.\n\nTables are stored in Parquet files, which you can also read in Python, R, MATLAB or DuckDB. Other Julia values are stored in JLD2 files. You choose the names and organization of the results within each run.","title":"OutputVault.jl"},{"category":"section","location":"#Installation","page":"Home","text":"using Pkg\nPkg.add(url = \"https://github.com/elenev/OutputVault.jl\")\nPkg.add(\"Parquet2\")\n\nRequires Julia 1.12 or later. Load Parquet2 when saving or loading tables.","title":"Installation"},{"category":"section","location":"#Quick-example","page":"Home","text":"using OutputVault, Parquet2\n\nset_config!(Config(output_root = mktempdir(),            # use a permanent folder for real runs\n                   table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat()))\n\nrf = RunHandle(\"base\"; branch = \"demo\", experdef = \"v1\")\nsolution = (params = (α = 0.33, β = 0.96), policy = [1.0, 1.1, 1.2])\nsave_solution(rf, solution)                                # starts the run over\nsave_result_item(rf, \"stats/uncond\", (variable = [\"K\", \"C\"], mean = [3.5, 1.2]))\nload_result_item(rf, \"stats/uncond\")                       # the table, with its metadata\n\nshape = QueryShape(\"stats/{sample}\"; keys = (:variable,), column = :stat)\nquery_results(RunPattern(experdef = \"v1\"), shape; stat = :mean)   # every run of v1, in long form\n\nThe query returns one row per selected cell, with columns branch, experdef, variant, sample, variable, stat, value. Saving another variant at the same item paths makes it part of the same query. Plain Julia values and Tables.jl tables can be saved directly; for your own types, see Storing Your Own Types.\n\nThe explicit branch chooses a folder, without switching git branches. Normally omit it to use the active project's branch. Provenance always records the active project's repository; without a repository, saving warns and still works. Saving a solution replaces the whole run, including previous result items.","title":"Quick example"},{"category":"section","location":"#Where-to-go-next","page":"Home","text":"Quick Start: save, load and compare two runs.\nRuns and Storage: runs, paths, the storage rules and provenance.\nSaving and Loading: configuration, solutions, items, finding runs.\nStoring Your Own Types: storage methods for your package's result types.\nTable Formats: Parquet, and adding a format.\nQueries: shapes, selections, and the SQL macros for other languages.","title":"Where to go next"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Runs-and-Storage","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"","title":"Runs and Storage"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Runs","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"A run is the saved results of one configuration of a model, in one folder:\n\n<output_root>/<branch>/res-<experdef>-<variant>/\n├── native.jld2       the solution as Julia objects (optional)\n├── .provenance/      git_commit.txt  [git_staged.diff  git_unstaged.diff]\n├── solution/ …       the solution, by the storage rules\n└── <result items> …  saved at paths chosen by the caller\n\nPart Meaning\noutput root the top folder of a project's runs (Config(; output_root))\nbranch the git branch the run belongs to; may contain /, which gives nested folders\nexperdef the experiment definition: a set of runs designed together\nvariant one configuration within an experdef\n\nA RunHandle names a run. The variant may contain ASCII letters, digits and _ . + = -; the experiment name may contain the same characters except -. A branch may contain /, but none of its levels may start with res- or ..\n\nThe branch defaults to the active Julia project's git branch (current_branch). The recorded commit and diffs also come from that project's repository, even if you start Julia in another folder. Pass branch explicitly when the project is outside a repository or on a detached HEAD.\n\nA handle only identifies a run; it creates no files. Changing the active configuration changes which output root that same handle refers to. Passing branch chooses a folder and does not switch git branches or change the source of provenance.\n\nA run starts with its solution. save_solution replaces the whole run, so a run never mixes results computed from two solutions. Result items are then saved with save_result_item, at any time and in any order, at paths chosen when the code runs. The top-level names solution and native are reserved; the git record is kept separately in .provenance/.","title":"Runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Paths","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"An item's path is relative to the run folder, with levels separated by / (\"stats\", \"irf/ss/stats\"). Each level is a nonempty file name without /, \\ or .. The file extension is added by the storage rules.","title":"Paths"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Storage-rules","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"OutputVault writes a value v at path p as follows.\n\nIt computes s = to_storage(v) (to_storage; v itself unless a package says otherwise).\nIf s is a table, it writes one table file p.<ext> with the configured table format and the table's own file-level metadata (DataAPI.metadata). A NamedTuple of equal-length vectors is a table; a NamedTuple of vectors of unequal lengths is an error naming the path. Writing a table without a table format in the configuration is an error.\nElse, if s is a NamedTuple, it writes a folder p/, with each member m at p/m by these same rules, to_storage included.\nOtherwise it writes p.jld2 with write_portable_jld2: the Julia object, unless the type's package defines its own layout.\n\nSaving at a path replaces what is there, in every form (p.<ext>, p.jld2, p/), so saving stats replaces the whole folder, including samples saved separately as stats/boom; to add one sample, save stats/boom. Saving below an item that is a file (irf/tfp when irf.jld2 exists) is an error. Each item is written to a temporary name next to it and then renamed into place, so readers never see half an item. The old item is renamed aside first and deleted last, so a save that fails leaves it as it was. On Windows, an old copy holding a file that is still open cannot be deleted: it stays beside the new item under a hidden name, and a warning names it.","title":"Storage rules"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Reading-returns-data","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"OutputVault returns the following data when no type is supplied:\n\na table file is a StoredTable, its columns with the file's metadata;\na folder is a NamedTuple of its items, in alphabetical order;\na .jld2 file is a Dict of its contents.\n\nTo restore an object of your own type, use load_result_item(rf, path, T). Define from_storage(T, data) for a table or folder, or read_portable_jld2(T, io) for a .jld2 file; see Storing Your Own Types.\n\nJLD2 reconstructs the Julia objects stored in its files, so reading them may require the original types and compatible code. For files that must also be read by other languages or later code, define a layout of ordinary arrays, numbers and strings.","title":"Reading returns data"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Provenance","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"save_solution records the git state of the repository of the active project (Base.active_project()), whatever the working directory, in .provenance/:\n\ngit_commit.txt: the commit, and one line submodule <path> <commit> per submodule;\nif the repository is dirty, git_staged.diff and git_unstaged.diff. The unstaged diff includes untracked text files, up to 1 MB in total (one line in the diff says when the rest was left out); binary files are skipped. Submodules contribute their commits, not their diffs.\n\ndirty_repo_check (:warn, :error or :ignore) says what to do when the repository is dirty or when there is no repository (then git_commit.txt says so). The repository's index is never written. Result items record no provenance: they belong to the solution of their run.\n\nOutputVault does not lock runs. Coordinate writers to the same run yourself; separate runs can be written independently. Commit the project's Manifest.toml when the recorded commit should also identify the package versions used.","title":"Provenance"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/concepts/#Reading-older-runs","page":"Runs and Storage","text":"Runs saved with OutputVault 0.3 load without changes. Their provenance is in solution/ and their native copy is solution/res.jld2. Use load_result_item(rf, \"solution\"; native = true) to read that copy. Runs saved with OutputVault 0.1 are not supported.","title":"Reading older runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Saving-and-Loading","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"","title":"Saving and Loading"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Configuration","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"Choose an output folder and a table format with set_config!. Subsequent saves, loads and queries use these settings:\n\nusing OutputVault, Parquet2\n\nset_config!(Config(output_root = \"/path/to/outputs\",\n                   table_format = OutputVault.Formats.ParquetFormat(),   # needed only to write tables\n                   native = false))                                      # skip native.jld2\n\nUse with_config to save to a different folder for a block of code:\n\nwith_config(Config(output_root = mktempdir())) do\n    trial = RunHandle(\"trial\"; branch = \"scratch\")\n    save_solution(trial, (; policy = [1.0, 1.1]))   # no tables, so no table format needed\nend\n\nThe override applies within the block and to tasks it starts. Other tasks keep their settings. Object files outside runs need no configuration.\n\nFor project-wide defaults, call set_config! when your project initializes. If you need to compute settings dynamically, such as choosing a folder for the current machine, define a subtype of OutputVault.AbstractConfig.\n\nstaging_root defaults to <output_root>/.staging. Keep it on the same file system as the output folder so completed runs can be renamed into place.","title":"Configuration"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Solutions","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"save_solution(rf, (; obj, stv))                     # solution/obj.jld2, solution/stv.jld2, native.jld2\nsave_solution(rf, (; obj, stv); dirty_repo_check = :error)\n\nsave_solution replaces the whole run, including previously saved result items, and returns the new run's folder. Choose a different variant name when you want to keep the previous solution.\n\nThe new run is written in a staging folder before replacing the old one. If it cannot be moved into place, save_solution returns its staging path and warns where both copies are. Follow the warning's instructions before adding more results; see Files held open on Windows.\n\nThe native copy, native.jld2, holds each member of the solution NamedTuple under its name:\n\nload_result_item(rf, \"solution\"; native = true)     # (; obj, stv) as Julia objects, read with JLD2\nload_result_item(rf, \"solution\")                    # stored data; here a NamedTuple of Dicts\nload_result_item(rf, \"solution/obj\", T; context)    # one part restored as a T\n\nnative = true bypasses storage hooks and needs the Julia types and compatible code that wrote the file. Without it, .jld2 items still contain Julia objects unless their package defines a portable layout. For a solution that is not a NamedTuple, the native loader returns (solution = value,).","title":"Solutions"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Result-items","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"save_result_item(rf, \"stats/uncond\", table)         # a table file\nsave_result_item(rf, \"irf/ss\", (; stats, paths))    # a folder of two tables\nload_result_item(rf, \"irf/ss\")                      # (; paths = StoredTable, stats = StoredTable)\nload_result_item(rf, \"irf/ss\", MyIRF; context)      # from_storage(MyIRF, data; context)\nmove_result_item(rf, \"irf/irf\", \"irf/ss/stats\")     # moved unchanged\ndelete_result_item(rf, \"irf/old\")\n\nThe run must exist, so its solution must have been saved. Moving onto an existing item, or moving or deleting the solution or a reserved name, is an error. list_run shows what a run holds:\n\nc = list_run(rf)\nc.provenance        # (commit = \"…\", dirty = false)\nc.items             # a column table: path, kind (:table, :jld2, :folder), format","title":"Result items"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Finding-runs","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"find_runs lists runs from folder names alone, so no file is opened or downloaded:\n\nfind_runs()                                              # the runs of top-level branches\nfind_runs(RunPattern(branch = \"**\", experdef = \"v1\"))    # nested branches too\nfind_runs(RunPattern(variant = \"frm*\"); root = \"/other/root\")\n\nA RunPattern has one glob per part of a run's name: * matches within one level, ? one character, [...] one character of a set, and ** any number of levels.","title":"Finding runs"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Objects-outside-a-run","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"write_object_file and read_object_file save and restore one object in a .jld2 file anywhere, through the same JLD2 hooks as items:\n\nwrite_object_file(\"obj.jld2\", obj)\nread_object_file(\"obj.jld2\", T; context)","title":"Objects outside a run"},{"category":"section","location":"guide/saving_loading/#Files-held-open-on-Windows","page":"Saving and Loading","text":"A file that is open, or memory-mapped, cannot be renamed or deleted on Windows. Parquet2 keeps the files of tables it has read mapped until Julia's garbage collector frees them (GC.gc()); a file opened by other code, or a shell or Julia session whose working directory is inside the run, also holds it. When such a file blocks replacing a run, no run is lost or partly deleted:\n\nif the old run cannot be renamed aside, it stays where it is, the new run stays in the staging folder, and save_solution returns the staging folder with a warning that says where both are;\nif only the delete of the old run fails, the new run is in place and a warning names the old folder (.trash-… next to the run) to delete by hand.","title":"Files held open on Windows"}]
}
